Collapse the Ruby pin to one file — and it had to be the other one

Two files pinned one version, and `smoke.yml` carried a step whose whole job was
checking they agreed. Now there is one file and no step.

**But it collapsed the opposite way to the plan, and you should know why before
merging.** The plan was *keep `.tool-versions`, drop `.ruby-version`* — your
*"we don't need ruby-version sitting around in root"*. I said that was blocked on
one fact I could not check. I checked it, and it came back against us twice.

## Fact one: Cloudflare reads `.ruby-version`, and never read `.tool-versions`

Their build image documents exactly three ways to set Ruby — the
`.ruby-version` file, the `RUBY_VERSION` environment variable, and a build
variable on the dashboard. `.tool-versions` is not among them; the only version
files they list are for Node and Python.

Their current default is **Ruby 3.4.4**. So deleting `.ruby-version` would not
have failed the build — it would have quietly moved the host onto a different
Ruby from CI and from local, with nothing left to notice. That is worse than
failing.

## Fact two: your link. `.tool-versions` is an active hazard there

The write-up you sent is the decisive one, and it is worse than "unsupported":

> the mere presence of this file in your project will break your build on
> Cloudflare, without any way to turn off this behavior

Cloudflare's builder *detects* `.tool-versions` undocumentedly and can fail the
whole build with `Failed: error occurred while installing tools or
dependencies`. `SKIP_DEPENDENCY_INSTALL` does not help. The published workaround
is to rename the file and set versions through build variables; **deleting it is
the same fix with one fewer file.**

So the file that had to survive is the one you named as expendable — and the
collapse is also the removal of a hazard. Not a bust at all.

## What it cost, and what pays for it

asdf reads `.ruby-version` only when `legacy_version_file = yes` is set in
`~/.asdfrc`, which is per-user and off by default. So without something in the
way, a contributor at the root gets whatever Ruby their shell hands them and
finds out from a confusing Jekyll error — the exact failure the deleted CI step
existed to catch.

**The `Gemfile` now carries `ruby ">= 3.2"`.** A floor, deliberately not a second
pin, so bumping `.ruby-version` stays a one-file change. Measured here against
the Ruby macOS ships:

```
$ /usr/bin/bundle check
Your Ruby version is 2.6.10, but your Gemfile specified >= 3.2
```

One line, names the version, refuses before Jekyll is reached. **That is a better
guard than the CI step it replaces**, because it fires where the mistake is made
rather than one push later.

## Also recorded

- **`docs/running-it.md`** — one pin, the `~/.asdfrc` line for anyone who wants
  asdf to keep selecting Ruby automatically, and the fact that skipping it is
  safe now. Bumping off end-of-life 3.2.2 is a one-file change now, and worth
  noting that Cloudflare's own default is already 3.4.4.
- **`docs/deploying.md`** — the two facts with their sources, and the guess above
  them left in place rather than quietly corrected, because the reasoning is the
  useful part.
- **A third thing to rule out** added to *When the git build fails*: this
  repository carried a `.tool-versions` from 2026-09-09 until today. It does
  **not** explain either failure on record — neither error matches, and the
  `Gemfile` failure predates the file — but it is the cheapest thing to check if
  a build ever dies in the tool-detection phase, and the variable is gone now.

## Verified

Builds; all thirteen check suites pass. The only behaviour change outside the
documentation is that `bundle` now refuses Ruby below 3.2 with a clear message.

Sources: [Cloudflare build image](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/ci-cd/builds/build-image/) · [Cloudflare Workers choke on asdf .tool-versions](https://www.codejam.info/2026/02/cloudflare-workers-choke-asdf-tool-versions.html)
